From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yevhen Salitrynskyi <ysalitrynskyi@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 21:45:58 -0400
Subject: hide the tabs that belong to another Space

The view half of ADR 0015. SpaceTabVisibilityController observes the window's
SpaceModel and hides tab views outside the active Space with SetVisible(false),
which is exactly how a collapsed tab group hides its own tabs. Nothing else has
to change: every tab container already skips children that
LayoutManagerBase::CanBeVisible() rejects, and that reflects SetVisible() calls
made from outside layout.

Showing a tab again asks the tab strip model whether a collapsed group is also
hiding it, so the two reasons a tab can be invisible do not fight each other.

The controller walks into group and folder views rather than only the top level,
because those hold tabs of their own.

Only normal windows have a SpaceModel, so in a popup or an app window the
controller is never created and every tab shows, as before.

Six tests cover the membership rules: a tab joins the active Space when first
asked, stays put when the active Space changes, a new tab joins whichever Space
is active, a tab can be moved, a move to a Space that does not exist is refused,
and a tab the model has never seen counts as visible. All 15 SpaceModelTest
tests pass in the real unit_tests binary.

Why: without this, Spaces are bookkeeping the user cannot see.
Removable when: the tab collection tree can express a hidden subtree itself.
